   "The work follows the local geography. Suburbs like Hampton, Studd Hill, Greenhill, Eddington and clifftop Beltinge carry mature amenity trees, while the Downs above the seafront, Bishopstone Glen and the vast Blean woodland complex arching over Canterbury, including Woodland Trust ground at Victory Wood, hold woodland and parkland. West Blean and Thornden Woods, a 490-hectare block of ancient oak and beech managed by Kent Wildlife Trust, the Forestry Commission's Clowes Wood and the mature avenues of Herne Bay Memorial Park add further canopy, with parts of the Blean coppiced in rotation and grazed by Highland cattle and Konik ponies, and much of the complex designated a Site of Special Scientific Interest and Special Area of Conservation threaded by a near-thousand-year-old droveway, all of it generating crown reductions, dismantles, felling, deadwooding and stump grinding for local firms.",
   "It is a safety-critical trade run to recognised standards. Herne Bay tree surgeons are typically NPTC, City and Guilds and LANTRA qualified, working to BS 3998 for tree work and BS 5837 where trees meet construction, many of them Arboricultural Association members, and ash dieback runs hard through the ash-rich Blean while TPO and conservation-area rules shape much of the in-town work. Every chainsaw job, on the ground or roped into a canopy, depends on cut-protective trousers, boots, a forestry helmet and gloves rated to EN ISO 11393.",
   "Run as small outfits for the most part: a climber and a groundsman, a family business, a one or two-van team working across Herne Bay, Whitstable, Canterbury and the surrounding Kent coast. They buy their own kit, get the sizes and protection class right and want it ordered direct, without a procurement department or a trade account in the way.",
